    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d9fec409_aa33463f",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/autocert.go",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 253,
      "author": {
        "id": 5065
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-12T00:35:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "since this is so easy for people to pass in as their fallback value, I wonder if a better default for a nil fallback is redirecting http to https, at least for GET/HEAD requests?\n\nI\u0027m curious what others think.\n\nAlso, if LetsEncrypt is shutting down tls-sni-{01,02} for good, maybe we should do even more magic in this package.\n\nIf this HTTPHandler method was never called, we could optimistically try to open port 80 and hope for the best and use it if we get it? The risk is that there\u0027s a firewall or proxies in front of us and port 80 requests never get to us. But it might fix a good chunk of people without any changes to their code.\n\nI also wonder whether we could add something to net/http + the standard library to route these http-01 ACME challenges to the autocert or autocert-like packages, without the user explicitly wiring them. I\u0027m thinking of a pretty safe way to do it, repeating such requests if the default handler ends up replying with a 404.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 253,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 253,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "0e3247d9797d99fea8011d1def1515ad37a803f8",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9554f1a9_0aad8e2a",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/autocert.go",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 253,
      "author": {
        "id": 6347
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-12T09:53:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "personally, I wouldn\u0027t mind 302-redirecting to https preserving both path and query, but it seems there are different opinions at https://github.com/golang/go/issues/21890.\n\nby \"we could optimistically try to open port 80\" you mean start a new listener on :80? that\u0027s an interesting idea! I wouldn\u0027t care that much about firewall and proxies. The challenge will just fail in that case and we can move on. I\u0027m more concerned with autocert occupying the port which otherwise might be in use by others like nginx or apache, whoever gets to it first on start/boot.\n\nI think I get what you\u0027re saying about routing http-01 in net/http, but need to think about it more. sounds like another interesting idea. also, don\u0027t know why but your original proposal in https://github.com/golang/go/issues/17053 came to mind.",
      "parentUuid": "d9fec409_aa33463f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 253,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 253,
        "endChar": 35
      },
